<pdd-dependency>pdf_engine_Python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

<include>prompts/contract_Python.prompt</include>
<include>prompts/session_store_Python.prompt</include>

Write the FastAPI application for the Zuzu orchestrator. An ElevenLabs
conversational agent calls these endpoints live while an applicant is on the
phone, so the read and write paths must stay in the tens of milliseconds.

Every endpoint except `/health` depends on `require_shared_secret`.

Requirements:

1. `GET /health` returns `{"status": "ok", "form_ids": [...]}` with no auth.

2. `POST /session/init` takes a `SessionInitRequest`, creates a session keyed by
   `conversation_id` (the session id everywhere in this system is the
   ElevenLabs `conversation_id`), publishes a `session_started` event, and returns
   a `SessionInitResponse`. In this milestone there is no persistent memory yet:
   return `applicant_name` empty, `is_returning` false, `preferred_language` "en",
   `active_form` the default form id, and a `known_summary` of "". The mem0
   lookup replaces this body in a later milestone and must not change the shape.

3. `POST /tools/get_missing_fields` resolves the form through the registry,
   computes the next missing field and the counts, and returns a
   `GetMissingFieldsResponse`. When nothing is missing, `next_field` is null --
   that is the agent's signal to call generate_form. Return 404 for an unknown
   form id and 404 for an unknown session id.

4. `POST /tools/save_field` validates that `field_id` exists in the form schema,
   stores the value, publishes a `field_saved` event, and returns immediately.
   **This path must not call an LLM, touch the filesystem, or build a PDF.**
   Set `needs_confirmation` true when the field is marked `sensitive` or the
   supplied `confidence` is below 0.85. Reject an unknown `field_id` with 422
   rather than storing it -- a value we cannot place on the form is a value we
   must not pretend to have collected.

5. `POST /tools/generate_form` computes missing required fields first. If any
   remain, return `status="incomplete"` with the list and no pdf_url. Otherwise
   fill the PDF, store the path on the session, publish a `form_ready` event, and
   return `status="complete"` with a `pdf_url` pointing at the download route.
   Run the fill in a worker thread so the event loop keeps serving the call.

6. `GET /forms/{session_id}.pdf` streams the generated PDF for that session with
   `application/pdf`, 404 when it does not exist yet.

7. `POST /session/complete` reconciles any values in `collected` that are not
   already stored, publishes `session_completed`, and returns the count of fields
   reconciled.

8. `GET /ws/{session_id}` is a WebSocket that subscribes to the event bus for
   that session and forwards each event as JSON. Authenticate it from a
   `secret` query parameter using `verify_secret`, closing with code 1008 when it
   does not match. Handle client disconnect without logging a stack trace.

9. `POST /demo/run` drives a full scripted call against this same contract using
   a preloaded demo persona and returns the resulting session id and pdf_url.
   This is the Demo Mode fallback: it must work with no microphone, no voice
   widget, and no network egress.

10. Configure permissive CORS for the dashboard origin. Add structured JSON
    logging that always includes `session_id` and **never** logs a field value;
    log field ids only. Log a duration in milliseconds for save_field and
    get_missing_fields so the latency claim is measured rather than asserted.

11. Type hints throughout. Keep route bodies thin -- orchestration only, with the
    real work delegated to the store, bus, and PDF modules.
